          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. Does the classification link the two, and what target does guidance describe for someone like me?",
          "answer": "Yes, the coding system links the two. In ICD-10-CM, high blood pressure that occurs with chronic kidney disease has its own combination code, I12, and the kidney stage is recorded as a second code. The convention treats them as linked unless the record says they are unrelated. This describes how records are coded, not a diagnosis for you (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*). The sources I could retrieve did not include the kidney staging definitions, so I can't confirm from them how stage 3 is defined.\n\nI could not find a blood pressure target for stage 3 kidney disease, because that search was cut off. The retrieved guidance does give these office-reading categories for adults: normal is top number below 120 and bottom below 80; elevated is 120 to 129 and bottom below 80; stage 1 is 130 to 139 or bottom 80 to 89; stage 2 is 140 or higher or bottom 90 or higher (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*). That same document says a treatment target is usually set once treatment begins, but the passage did not state it.\n\nOther retrieved guidance addresses kidney disease in treatment decisions. It lists chronic kidney disease among the features that make drug treatment appropriate at stage 1 hypertension. Without those features, it suggests lifestyle changes first and reassessment after three to six months. It also says a renin-angiotensin blocker is preferred when chronic kidney disease is present, especially with protein in the urine. The coronary artery disease guidance gives a target below 130/80 mm Hg for most people with that condition, but it is not written for kidney disease, so I can't say whether it applies to you. The sources do not agree on a kidney-specific target, and I could not retrieve the section where authorities disagree.\n\nIf you have very high readings along with chest pain, severe headache, confusion, weakness on one side, or trouble breathing, seek emergency care right away. Otherwise, ask your kidney or primary care clinician which blood pressure target applies to you, given your kidney function, any protein in your urine, and your other conditions. Ask them which guideline they are following, and whether your current plan matches it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
